u have to be a little bit  lucky but the luck ran out quickly after  robin hood  a popular app among armchair investors  pulled the plug  and stopped more people from buying  gamestop stock  the sec is reviewing why it's  infuriating  they're basically saying we'll let the  big boys keep playing but the little  guys have to sit this one out now  some believe robin hood was trying to  protect the hedge funds that were losing  billions  robin hood also restricted trading on  blackberry and bed bath and beyond  many lawmakers were furious all of a  sudden the billionaires and some hedge  funds  are yelling because they're not the ones  the only ones  who make money when the manipulation  works the head of robin hood  denies any wrongdoing we absolutely did  not do  this at the direction of any market  maker  or hedge fund or anyone we route to or  other market participants  some small investors made big bucks in  the revolt trying to get rich quick  others say they just wanted to stick it  to wall street elites this is a chance  to get the  five five five and they got enough  people we don't fully understand all the  intentions right now  experts are warning this kind of buying  is risky because any stock can drop  dramatically in a matter of seconds  but some young investors didn't seem to  care it's the regular joe's versus wall  street  and that's what when i put money into it  i said even if this goes to zero and i  lose everything  i like being a part of it whether the  stock surge will help gamestop survive  this pandemic is unknown  but its three biggest individual  shareholders are now billionaires  jennifer johnson global news washington  and a ten-year-old boy from texas has  cashed in from gamestop  two years ago jayden carr's mother  bought him 10 shares to teach her son  about economics  she paid 60 u.s at the time well this  budding broker has learned a thing or  two and he sold his stock  for 3 200 american  still ahead general motors electric  shift and how it will drive canada's  auto industry  automakers have long been told the only  way to drive the industry forward  is to reverse emissions and phase out  fossil fuels for more environmentally  sustainable options  general motors is aiming to go all  electric the company will end the  production of gas and diesel-powered  cars and trucks by 2035  and turn completely carbon neutral by  2040.  as eric sorensen reports the shift is  impacting canada's  auto manufacturing industry  for more than a hundred years fossil  fuels have been the lifeblood of the  combustion engines built by general  motors  but in a world of undeniable climate  change pressure to choke off tail pipe  emissions  that cause greenhouse gases is turning  gm  green most of its cars will be electric  vehicles by 2035.  it comes on top of gm's plans to invest  about 35 billion dollars in electric  and autonomous vehicles over the next  five years  gm ceo calls the historic moment an  inflection point  the moment when our world's reliance on  gas and diesel-powered vehicles  will begin transitioning to an  all-electric future  e-vehicles represent just 3 percent of  the world's vehicle market  but gm's move could spur a chain  reaction of industries to turn greener  faster it's going to have all kinds of  impacts along  not just general motors and its massive  supply chain but in terms of moving the  whole  goal posts towards a ev future and a  carbon neutral future  the old expression what's good for gm is  good for the country  may now be what's good for the planet  electric vehicles  represent an important climate solution  but also an important economic solution  when you look at major auto  manufacturers such as dm  making commitments to have zero tailpipe  emissions by 2035  that's significant in canada gm is  undertaking a billion dollar  transformation of this ontario plant  from producing the gas drinking equinox  to new electric delivery vehicles for  companies like fedex that's where the  industry is heading  that's where consumers are heading  they're not there today but they will be  and canada also sees it as an incredible  opportunity to create jobs  but among the cascading effects of gm  changing gears  is potentially another blow to canada's  oil and gas economy  large fossil fuel producing provinces  the writing is on the wall  and they need to start thinking about  how they're going to transition out of  the  oil economy gm plans to release 30 new  e-vehicles by 2025  and that still leaves room to go big in  car buying an e-powered humvee  is coming out in the next year eric  sorensen global news toronto  the model turned role model for  generations of black women  up next the remarkable life of cicely  tyson  he'll know when to call me  when they call me i'll be ready  hollywood legend  cicely tyson has died tyson got her  start as a model  that helped launch a television movie  and broadway career spanning more than  six decades  she became one of the first black actors  in a recurring tv  role in east side west side  her role as the sharecropper's wife and  sounder earned her an oscar nomination  she won two emmys for the 1974  television  drama the autobiography of miss jane  pittman  a new generation of moviegoers witnessed  her talent in the hit  the help in 2013 she won a tony award  at the age of 88. tyson helped shatter  hollywood's stereotypes by insisting she  played characters  that allowed her to radiate dignity and  strength  in 2016 she was given the presidential  medal of freedom  the highest civilian honor in the united  states  and just this week she released a memoir  called just as i am  tyson was 96.  across the country canadians are marking  a somber anniversary it has been four  years since the deadly shooting at a  mosque in quebec city  most of today's memorials are taking  place online  including the green square campaign  supporters wear a green square  representing the green carpets at the  mosque  as a display of unity on this day in  2017  six men were killed when a gunman opened  fire at the quebec city islamic cultural  center  it remains the worst mass murder in a  house of worship in canadian history  tonight we leave you with more of the  touching tributes to the victims  good night  you
 

